WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:05.720
Now that we understand this idea of an exponent, we want to look at arithmetic with terms that involve

00:05.720 --> 00:06.260
exponents.

00:06.260 --> 00:10.830
So just as a reminder, let's say that we have this exponential term here.

00:10.850 --> 00:14.870
Remember, we call X, the base two is the exponent.

00:14.900 --> 00:20.620
Now we want to recognize that we can also have what's called a coefficient in front of the base.

00:20.630 --> 00:23.030
This three here is acting as the coefficient.

00:23.060 --> 00:27.460
It just multiplies the x squared or the x to the power of two.

00:27.470 --> 00:32.840
In other words, this is the same here as x squared.

00:33.530 --> 00:35.060
Plus X squared.

00:35.710 --> 00:42.070
Plus X squared, because here we're saying we have three X squared and here we're saying we have one

00:42.070 --> 00:42.640
x squared.

00:42.640 --> 00:45.580
If we add another one to it, we have two x squared.

00:45.580 --> 00:48.010
And if we add another one to it, we have three x squared.

00:48.010 --> 00:50.680
And then this is how we write three x squared.

00:50.680 --> 00:58.090
So with this idea of the coefficient, the base and the exponent, let's look at how to add two terms

00:58.090 --> 00:59.740
that include exponents.

00:59.740 --> 01:02.470
So here we have three x squared plus x squared.

01:02.470 --> 01:08.230
Whenever there's no coefficient, there's essentially an implied coefficient of positive one.

01:08.230 --> 01:12.460
So this is actually saying three x squared plus one x squared.

01:12.460 --> 01:17.320
Well when we have three of them and we're adding another one of them, we're just going to get four

01:17.320 --> 01:17.680
of them.

01:17.680 --> 01:23.470
So we're going to have four x squared is the result of this addition.

01:23.470 --> 01:26.890
We can also subtract terms with exponents.

01:26.890 --> 01:31.090
So if instead of the addition here, we use subtraction.

01:31.120 --> 01:34.660
Three of them, minus one of them is two of them.

01:34.660 --> 01:37.340
So we end up with two x squared.

01:37.370 --> 01:43.160
Now, it's important to say here that in order to do addition or subtraction with terms that include

01:43.160 --> 01:46.580
exponents, we have to have equivalent bases.

01:46.580 --> 01:50.750
So the bases have to be the same and the exponents have to be the same.

01:50.990 --> 01:55.850
So in both of these we were able to do the addition and subtraction because we had equivalent bases

01:55.880 --> 01:58.400
x and equivalent exponents.

01:58.400 --> 02:00.020
Two Same thing down here.

02:00.020 --> 02:03.140
Both bases were x, both exponents were two.

02:03.170 --> 02:05.510
So we were able to do the addition and subtraction.

02:05.510 --> 02:11.720
The coefficients do not have to be the same because the coefficients just tell us how many of that term

02:11.720 --> 02:12.470
we have.

02:12.500 --> 02:23.180
So to give a counter example, if we had maybe three x squared minus four x, we could not simplify

02:23.180 --> 02:29.780
this expression or combine these terms in any way because while the bases are the same, they both have

02:29.780 --> 02:33.470
base X, we have different coefficients, three and four, that's fine.

02:33.470 --> 02:36.860
But they have the same base, they have different exponents.

02:36.860 --> 02:42.110
Here we have an exponent of two here we have an implied exponent of one.

02:42.140 --> 02:43.580
The exponents are different.

02:43.580 --> 02:45.770
And so these are not like terms.

02:45.770 --> 02:47.660
We can't combine them in any way.

02:47.660 --> 02:53.480
We can't simplify this expression now while we have to have equivalent bases and equivalent exponents

02:53.480 --> 02:57.590
for addition and subtraction for multiplication and division.

02:57.710 --> 03:03.650
It is true that the bases have to be equivalent, but the exponents can be different.

03:03.650 --> 03:07.550
So here we're able to do this multiplication because we have equivalent bases.

03:07.550 --> 03:08.780
The bases are both x.

03:08.810 --> 03:11.990
The fact that the exponents are different doesn't matter.

03:11.990 --> 03:19.820
So the result of this is going to be just x, we keep the same base and then we add the exponents.

03:19.820 --> 03:27.170
So we get x to the two plus three or x to the fifth, x to the five.

03:27.170 --> 03:28.970
And the reason that that's true.

03:29.590 --> 03:35.860
Is because if we expand both the x squared and x cubed here, if we write them out as their expanded

03:35.860 --> 03:42.670
product, we know that x squared is the same as x times x, then we're multiplying here.

03:42.760 --> 03:48.250
We know x cubed is the same as x times x times x.

03:48.490 --> 03:54.610
And so what we see then here when we expand both of these is that we have five factors of X multiplied

03:54.610 --> 04:00.550
together and we know from exponents that we can rewrite that as X to the five.

04:00.580 --> 04:02.890
So that's how we're actually getting this result.

04:02.890 --> 04:05.710
That's how we justify that we can add the exponents.

04:05.710 --> 04:10.480
And in a more general sense we call that the product rule for exponents.

04:10.480 --> 04:19.240
And it tells us that any X to the A multiplied by any X to the B, again, here the bases are the same

04:19.510 --> 04:22.560
and the exponents can be different.

04:22.570 --> 04:23.140
By the way.

04:23.140 --> 04:25.420
The exponents can also be the same here.

04:25.420 --> 04:30.410
But this tells us that they're allowed to be different when we have any product like this.

04:30.440 --> 04:35.720
We know by the product rule for exponents that the result is going to be X to the A plus B.

04:35.750 --> 04:43.490
Now we also have a related rule for exponents that tells us that when the bases are different but the

04:43.490 --> 04:48.320
exponents are the same and we're multiplying, we can rewrite that product.

04:48.320 --> 04:56.450
This way we can multiply the bases so A times B and then raise that whole thing to the power of N.

04:56.450 --> 05:03.140
So as an example, let's say here we have three squared multiplied by two squared.

05:03.140 --> 05:05.260
We can see there that the bases are different.

05:05.270 --> 05:08.000
The bases A and B are three and two.

05:08.030 --> 05:10.160
They're different, but the exponents are the same.

05:10.160 --> 05:12.800
We have an exponent of two in both cases.

05:12.800 --> 05:21.230
So this rule tells us that we can rewrite this as three times two, multiplying the bases and then raise

05:21.230 --> 05:23.390
that whole thing to the power of two.

05:23.390 --> 05:27.410
And we can see that that's going to be equal to three times two is six.

05:27.410 --> 05:32.990
So we get six squared and of course we know that that's equal to 36.

05:33.020 --> 05:41.330
Now, if we did this without using this rule, what we would do is evaluate each of these terms individually.

05:41.330 --> 05:45.320
We know that three squared is nine, we know that two squared is four.

05:45.320 --> 05:47.690
So we get nine times four.

05:47.690 --> 05:55.250
And again, you can see we end up with the same value 36 And so we can see that this rule is in fact

05:55.250 --> 05:55.960
holding.

05:55.970 --> 06:02.240
So this product rule, this is multiplication of terms with exponents, but we also have a rule for

06:02.240 --> 06:03.980
dividing terms with exponents.

06:03.980 --> 06:07.430
So for example, here we have X to the fifth divided by x squared.

06:07.430 --> 06:13.880
In other words, we have a fraction where x to the fifth is the numerator and x squared is the denominator.

06:13.880 --> 06:20.000
Again, just like with multiplication, when we have this division of terms with exponents, the bases

06:20.000 --> 06:24.980
have to be equivalent, but the exponents can be different.

06:24.980 --> 06:30.560
And when we're dividing terms like this, the rule is that we subtract the exponents.

06:30.560 --> 06:35.900
So we keep the base the same, the base stays X, but then we take the exponent in the numerator and

06:35.900 --> 06:39.140
we subtract from that the exponent in the denominator.

06:39.140 --> 06:43.160
And the result then is x cubed in this case.

06:43.160 --> 06:45.140
And we can see that that's true.

06:45.140 --> 06:51.740
If we write this out X to the fifth over x squared, well x to the fifth we could write as x times x

06:51.740 --> 06:52.760
times x.

06:54.090 --> 06:54.960
This way.

06:54.960 --> 06:59.700
And then in the denominator we know x squared is x times x.

06:59.820 --> 07:06.300
And we saw before when we learned how to simplify fractions and identify equivalent fractions that when

07:06.300 --> 07:10.350
we have a common factor in the numerator and denominator, we can cancel it out.

07:10.380 --> 07:16.020
So we could cancel this factor of x with this one and then cancel this factor of x with this one.

07:16.020 --> 07:23.580
And you can see that all we're left with, all that remains is three factors of x here, which we know

07:23.580 --> 07:25.350
we could write as x cubed.

07:25.350 --> 07:28.290
And so you see how we get back to x cubed.

07:28.290 --> 07:31.950
That's the expanded version that justifies this rule.

07:32.130 --> 07:36.930
And if we want to generalize this rule, it's called the quotient rule for exponents.

07:36.930 --> 07:40.140
It governs division with exponential terms here.

07:40.140 --> 07:45.930
So again, this just tells us what we just learned, which is that we keep the base the same and we

07:45.960 --> 07:47.460
subtract the exponents.

07:47.460 --> 07:52.470
So we take the exponent in the numerator and subtract from that the exponent in the denominator.

07:52.470 --> 07:56.500
So again, these are some rules with exponents.

07:56.500 --> 08:02.530
Just remember that when we're adding or subtracting, the bases have to be the same and the exponent

08:02.530 --> 08:07.870
has to be the same in order for us to combine terms, in order for these to be like terms, in order

08:07.870 --> 08:09.280
to simplify the expression.

08:09.280 --> 08:13.930
But when we're multiplying or dividing, the bases still have to be the same.

08:13.930 --> 08:16.450
But the exponents can be different.
